[1mMCPIP — can you tell if someone deleted an audit record?[0m
[2mThe gateway signs a Merkle root per epoch. The key is not in Redis.[0m

[1m1. Verify the signed chain as it stands[0m
  [2m$[0m [36mcurl -s http://localhost:8080/v1/audit/verify[0m
  [32m{"intact":true,"first_bad_epoch":null}[0m

[1m2. Delete one sealed record — straight in Redis, bypassing the gateway[0m
  [2m$[0m [36mredis-cli -p 63790 XDEL mcpip:worm:events 1785960540041-0[0m
  (integer) 1

[1m3. Ask the gateway to verify the chain again[0m
  [2m$[0m [36mcurl -s http://localhost:8080/v1/audit/verify[0m
  [31m{"intact":false,"first_bad_epoch":6}[0m

[32m[1m✓ Caught.[0m [1mThe gateway names the epoch the missing record was in.[0m
[2mThe root is recomputed from the surviving records and compared against the one
Ed25519-signed at epoch close. Re-signing a root that matches what was left behind
needs the signing key, which is not in Redis — so deletion is detectable, not deniable.[0m

